            title: "Why Dynamic Memory",
            concept: "Dynamic memory is memory requested at run time with malloc, so a program can handle data whose size is only known while it runs. Stack arrays need a size at compile time, but malloc can allocate exactly as much as the input requires.",
            syntax: "int *a = malloc(n * sizeof(int));",
            exampleCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int total = 0;\n    for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) total += i;\n    printf(\"total: %d\\n\", total);\n    return 0;\n}\n",
