            bug: { prompt: "The code below opens a file but never closes it. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "FILE *f = fopen(\"data.txt\", \"r\");\nif (f == NULL) return 1;\nchar line[128];\nfgets(line, sizeof line, f);\nprintf(\"%s\", line);", fixedCode: "FILE *f = fopen(\"data.txt\", \"r\");\nif (f == NULL) return 1;\nchar line[128];\nfgets(line, sizeof line, f);\nprintf(\"%s\", line);\nfclose(f);", explanation: "Every successful fopen needs a matching fclose, otherwise the file descriptor and buffer leak." },
            exercise: { title: "Reverse a word for free and Ownership", type: "write-code", instructions: "Write a C program that prints a word reversed. This practises free and Ownership in Dynamic Memory: malloc and free: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *word = \"free_and_own\";\n    /* TODO: print the word reversed */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *word = \"free_and_own\";\n    size_t n = strlen(word);\n    for (size_t i = n; i > 0; i--) putchar(word[i - 1]);\n    putchar('\\n');\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program prints a word reversed and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Output", expectedStdout: "nwo_dna_eerf", hidden: false }, { name: "Result appears", expectedContains: "nwo_dna_eerf", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of free and Ownership: free returns memory obtained from malloc to the system, and after free the pointer must not be used again.", approach: "Work in small steps for free and Ownership: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for free and Ownership, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
