            exercise: { title: "Find the larger value for Growing an Array", type: "write-code", instructions: "Write a C program that reads two integers from standard input and prints the larger one. This practises Growing an Array in Dynamic Memory: malloc and free: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a, b;\n    if (scanf(\"%d %d\", &a, &b) != 2) return 1;\n    /* TODO: print the larger value */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a, b;\n    if (scanf(\"%d %d\", &a, &b) != 2) return 1;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", a > b ? a : b);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program reads two integers from standard input and prints the larger one and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "29 8\n", expectedStdout: "29", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "32 13\n", expectedStdout: "32", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Growing an Array: A dynamic array grows by allocating a bigger block, copying the old elements across, and freeing the old block.", approach: "Work in small steps for Growing an Array: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Growing an Array, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
