            title: "Dangling Pointers",
            concept: "A dangling pointer is a pointer that still points to memory after it has been freed, and using it reads or writes memory that may already belong to something else. Setting a pointer to NULL right after freeing it makes accidental use fail loudly instead of silently corrupting data.",
            syntax: "free(a); a = NULL;",
            exampleCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *word = \"example\";\n    printf(\"length of %s: %zu\\n\", word, strlen(word));\n    return 0;\n}\n",
