            exercise: { title: "Find the larger value for Resizing Strings", type: "test-driven", instructions: "Write a C program that reads two integers from standard input and prints the larger one. This practises Resizing Strings in realloc and Dynamic Arrays: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a, b;\n    if (scanf(\"%d %d\", &a, &b) != 2) return 1;\n    /* TODO: print the larger value */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a, b;\n    if (scanf(\"%d %d\", &a, &b) != 2) return 1;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", a > b ? a : b);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program reads two integers from standard input and prints the larger one and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "17 8\n", expectedStdout: "17", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "20 13\n", expectedStdout: "20", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Resizing Strings: A string buffer grows with realloc in the same way as any dynamic array, but the program must also keep room for the terminating NUL byte.", approach: "Work in small steps for Resizing Strings: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Resizing Strings, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
