            exercise: { title: "Sum of digits for Searching a List", type: "test-driven", instructions: "Write a C program that reads a positive integer from standard input and prints the sum of its digits. This practises Searching a List in Linked Lists: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    /* TODO: print the digit sum */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    int s = 0;\n    for (int x = n; x > 0; x /= 10) s += x % 10;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", s);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program reads a positive integer from standard input and prints the sum of its digits and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "137\n", expectedStdout: "11", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "139\n", expectedStdout: "13", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Searching a List: Searching a list walks from the head comparing each node's data with the target and stops at the first match or at NULL.", approach: "Work in small steps for Searching a List: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Searching a List, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
