            exercise: { title: "Greatest common divisor for What a Stack Is", type: "write-code", instructions: "Write a C program that reads two integers from standard input and prints their greatest common divisor. This practises What a Stack Is in Stacks: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a, b;\n    if (scanf(\"%d %d\", &a, &b) != 2) return 1;\n    /* TODO: print the gcd */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a, b;\n    if (scanf(\"%d %d\", &a, &b) != 2) return 1;\n    while (b != 0) { int t = a % b; a = b; b = t; }\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", a);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program reads two integers from standard input and prints their greatest common divisor and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "48 24\n", expectedStdout: "24", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "51 29\n", expectedStdout: "1", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of What a Stack Is: A stack is a collection where the last item added is the first item removed, which is called last-in, first-out order.", approach: "Work in small steps for What a Stack Is: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for What a Stack Is, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
