            exercise: { title: "Factorial for Stacks in Expression Evaluation", type: "test-driven", instructions: "Write a C program that reads an integer n from standard input and prints n factorial. This practises Stacks in Expression Evaluation in Stacks: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    /* TODO: print the factorial */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    int r = 1;\n    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) r *= i;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", r);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program reads an integer n from standard input and prints n factorial and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "7\n", expectedStdout: "5040", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "9\n", expectedStdout: "362880", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Stacks in Expression Evaluation: Evaluating expressions uses a stack to hold operators or operands while the expression is scanned from left to right.", approach: "Work in small steps for Stacks in Expression Evaluation: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Stacks in Expression Evaluation, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
