            exercise: { title: "Sum of digits for Freeing a Stack", type: "write-code", instructions: "Write a C program that reads a positive integer from standard input and prints the sum of its digits. This practises Freeing a Stack in Stacks: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    /* TODO: print the digit sum */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    int s = 0;\n    for (int x = n; x > 0; x /= 10) s += x % 10;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", s);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program reads a positive integer from standard input and prints the sum of its digits and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "100\n", expectedStdout: "1", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "102\n", expectedStdout: "3", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Freeing a Stack: Freeing a linked-list stack pops every node until the stack is empty, freeing each node as it goes.", approach: "Work in small steps for Freeing a Stack: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Freeing a Stack, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
