    capstoneExercise: { title: "Capstone coding task: Stacks", type: "build-program", instructions: "Write a complete C program applying what you learned in Stacks. It should reads two integers from standard input and prints the larger one. Print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a, b;\n    if (scanf(\"%d %d\", &a, &b) != 2) return 1;\n    /* TODO: print the larger value */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a, b;\n    if (scanf(\"%d %d\", &a, &b) != 2) return 1;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", a > b ? a : b);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program reads two integers from standard input and prints the larger one and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "29 8\n", expectedStdout: "29", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "32 13\n", expectedStdout: "32", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "The capstone for Stacks combines the track's ideas into one program: read the input, compute with a loop, and print only the result.", approach: "Work in three steps: read the input, compute the value, then print the result.", structure: "Keep the program in one main function with clearly initialised variables, and test it with the example input first." } },
