    capstoneExercise: { title: "Capstone coding task: Binary Trees", type: "build-program", instructions: "Write a complete C program applying what you learned in Binary Trees. It should reads an integer n from standard input and prints the nth Fibonacci number (F(0)=0, F(1)=1). Print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    /* TODO: print the Fibonacci number */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    int a = 0, b = 1;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { int t = a + b; a = b; b = t; }\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", a);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program reads an integer n from standard input and prints the nth Fibonacci number (F(0)=0, F(1)=1) and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "10\n", expectedStdout: "55", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "12\n", expectedStdout: "144", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "The capstone for Binary Trees combines the track's ideas into one program: read the input, compute with a loop, and print only the result.", approach: "Work in three steps: read the input, compute the value, then print the result.", structure: "Keep the program in one main function with clearly initialised variables, and test it with the example input first." } },
