            exercise: { title: "Sum of digits for Sorting with qsort", type: "write-code", instructions: "Write a C program that reads a positive integer from standard input and prints the sum of its digits. This practises Sorting with qsort in Sorting Algorithms: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    /* TODO: print the digit sum */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    int s = 0;\n    for (int x = n; x > 0; x /= 10) s += x % 10;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", s);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program reads a positive integer from standard input and prints the sum of its digits and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "248\n", expectedStdout: "14", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "250\n", expectedStdout: "7", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Sorting with qsort: The standard library function qsort sorts any array when given a comparator function that returns a negative, zero, or positive value.", approach: "Work in small steps for Sorting with qsort: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Sorting with qsort, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
