            title: "Tail Recursion",
            concept: "A recursive call is in tail position when it is the last thing the function does, so the current frame can be reused instead of kept. Tail recursion can run in constant stack space when the compiler optimises it, but C does not guarantee that optimisation.",
            syntax: "return f(n - 1, acc + n); /* tail call */",
            exampleCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    unsigned int x = 128;\n    unsigned int mask = 0x43;\n    printf(\"masked: %u\\n\", x & mask);\n    return 0;\n}\n",
            exampleLanguage: "c",
            mistake: "Assuming the compiler will always optimise a tail call away.",
            bug: { prompt: "The code below copies a string into a fixed buffer without checking its length. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "char buf[16];\nstrcpy(buf, input);", fixedCode: "char buf[16];\nstrncpy(buf, input, sizeof(buf) - 1);\nbuf[sizeof(buf) - 1] = '\\0';", explanation: "strcpy copies without a length limit and can overflow buf; strncpy with an explicit bound plus a terminating NUL keeps the copy inside the buffer." },
            exercise: { title: "Checksum of a word for Tail Recursion", type: "test-driven", instructions: "Write a C program that prints the checksum (sum of the character codes) of a word. This practises Tail Recursion in Recursive Algorithms: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *word = \"tail_recursi\";\n    /* TODO: print the checksum */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *word = \"tail_recursi\";\n    int total = 0;\n    for (size_t i = 0; word[i] != '\\0'; i++) total += (unsigned char)word[i];\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", total);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program prints the checksum (sum of the character codes) of a word and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Output", expectedStdout: "1286", hidden: false }, { name: "Result appears", expectedContains: "1286", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Tail Recursion: A recursive call is in tail position when it is the last thing the function does, so the current frame can be reused instead of kept.", approach: "Work in small steps for Tail Recursion: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Tail Recursion, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
