            exercise: { title: "Count the vowels for Recursion on Arrays", type: "write-code", instructions: "Write a C program that prints how many vowels a word contains. This practises Recursion on Arrays in Recursive Algorithms: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *word = \"recursion_on\";\n    /* TODO: print the vowel count */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *word = \"recursion_on\";\n    int count = 0;\n    for (size_t i = 0; word[i] != '\\0'; i++) {\n        char c = word[i];\n        if (c=='a'||c=='e'||c=='i'||c=='o'||c=='u') count++;\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", count);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program prints how many vowels a word contains and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Output", expectedStdout: "5", hidden: false }, { name: "Result appears", expectedContains: "5", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Recursion on Arrays: Recursion on an array usually processes the first element and recurses on the rest, or splits the array in half and recurses on each half.", approach: "Work in small steps for Recursion on Arrays: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Recursion on Arrays, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
