            exercise: { title: "Reverse a word for Checking Library Calls", type: "write-code", instructions: "Write a C program that prints a word reversed. This practises Checking Library Calls in Error Handling in C: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *word = \"checking_lib\";\n    /* TODO: print the word reversed */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *word = \"checking_lib\";\n    size_t n = strlen(word);\n    for (size_t i = n; i > 0; i--) putchar(word[i - 1]);\n    putchar('\\n');\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program prints a word reversed and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Output", expectedStdout: "bil_gnikcehc", hidden: false }, { name: "Result appears", expectedContains: "bil_gnikcehc", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Checking Library Calls: Library calls such as fopen, malloc and read can all fail, and each one documents what it returns on failure.", approach: "Work in small steps for Checking Library Calls: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Checking Library Calls, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
