            bug: { prompt: "The code below copies a string into a fixed buffer without checking its length. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "char buf[16];\nstrcpy(buf, input);", fixedCode: "char buf[16];\nstrncpy(buf, input, sizeof(buf) - 1);\nbuf[sizeof(buf) - 1] = '\\0';", explanation: "strcpy copies without a length limit and can overflow buf; strncpy with an explicit bound plus a terminating NUL keeps the copy inside the buffer." },
            exercise: { title: "Power of two for Handling Invalid Input", type: "test-driven", instructions: "Write a C program that reads an integer n from standard input and prints 2 to the power n. This practises Handling Invalid Input in Error Handling in C: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    /* TODO: print the power of two */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    int r = 1;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) r *= 2;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", r);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program reads an integer n from standard input and prints 2 to the power n and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "5\n", expectedStdout: "32", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "7\n", expectedStdout: "128", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Handling Invalid Input: Invalid input must be detected and rejected with a clear message instead of being processed as if it were valid.", approach: "Work in small steps for Handling Invalid Input: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Handling Invalid Input, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
