    capstoneExercise: { title: "Capstone coding task: Error Handling in C", type: "build-program", instructions: "Write a complete C program applying what you learned in Error Handling in C. It should reads two integers from standard input and prints their greatest common divisor. Print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a, b;\n    if (scanf(\"%d %d\", &a, &b) != 2) return 1;\n    /* TODO: print the gcd */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a, b;\n    if (scanf(\"%d %d\", &a, &b) != 2) return 1;\n    while (b != 0) { int t = a % b; a = b; b = t; }\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", a);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program reads two integers from standard input and prints their greatest common divisor and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "48 30\n", expectedStdout: "6", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "51 35\n", expectedStdout: "1", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "The capstone for Error Handling in C combines the track's ideas into one program: read the input, compute with a loop, and print only the result.", approach: "Work in three steps: read the input, compute the value, then print the result.", structure: "Keep the program in one main function with clearly initialised variables, and test it with the example input first." } },
    capstone: { title: "Error Handling in C capstone project", description: "Build a small working system that applies the ideas of Error Handling in C end to end, with clean C code, error handling, and tests.", requirements: ["Implement the core behaviour of Error Handling in C in C using only the standard library", "Handle invalid input and error cases without crashing", "Organise the code into functions with clear responsibilities", "Include a small test run that demonstrates the program working"], rubric: ["Core behaviour works correctly on the example inputs", "Error cases are handled gracefully", "Code is organised, readable, and free of memory errors", "The test run demonstrates the program end to end"] },
