    capstoneExercise: { title: "Capstone coding task: Multi-File Programs", type: "build-program", instructions: "Write a complete C program applying what you learned in Multi-File Programs. It should prints a word reversed. Print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *word = \"multifile_pr\";\n    /* TODO: print the word reversed */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *word = \"multifile_pr\";\n    size_t n = strlen(word);\n    for (size_t i = n; i > 0; i--) putchar(word[i - 1]);\n    putchar('\\n');\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program prints a word reversed and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Output", expectedStdout: "rp_elifitlum", hidden: false }, { name: "Result appears", expectedContains: "rp_elifitlum", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "The capstone for Multi-File Programs combines the track's ideas into one program: read the input, compute with a loop, and print only the result.", approach: "Work in three steps: read the input, compute the value, then print the result.", structure: "Keep the program in one main function with clearly initialised variables, and test it with the example input first." } },
