            title: "Phony Targets",
            concept: "A phony target is a target that is not a file, such as clean or all, and it must be declared with .PHONY so Make runs it even when a file of that name exists. Without the declaration, a stray file named clean would stop the rule from running.",
            syntax: ".PHONY: clean\nclean:\n\trm -f *.o app",
            exampleCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int total = 0;\n    for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) total += i;\n    printf(\"total: %d\\n\", total);\n    return 0;\n}\n",
            exampleLanguage: "c",
            mistake: "Forgetting .PHONY, so a file named clean blocks the clean target.",
            bug: { prompt: "The code below uses a variable before giving it a value. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "int total;\nfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) total += a[i];\nprintf(\"%d\\n\", total);", fixedCode: "int total = 0;\nfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) total += a[i];\nprintf(\"%d\\n\", total);", explanation: "An uninitialised local variable holds an indeterminate value, so total must be set to 0 before the loop accumulates into it." },
            exercise: { title: "Square a number for Phony Targets", type: "write-code", instructions: "Write a C program that reads an integer from standard input and prints its square. This practises Phony Targets in Make and Build Systems: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    /* TODO: print n squared */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", n * n);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program reads an integer from standard input and prints its square and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "8\n", expectedStdout: "64", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "10\n", expectedStdout: "100", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Phony Targets: A phony target is a target that is not a file, such as clean or all, and it must be declared with .PHONY so Make runs it even when a file of that name exists.", approach: "Work in small steps for Phony Targets: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Phony Targets, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
