            exercise: { title: "Length of a word for Why Test Your Code", type: "write-code", instructions: "Write a C program that prints the length of a word. This practises Why Test Your Code in Unit Testing in C: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *word = \"why_test_you\";\n    /* TODO: print the length */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *word = \"why_test_you\";\n    printf(\"%zu\\n\", strlen(word));\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program prints the length of a word and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Output", expectedStdout: "12", hidden: false }, { name: "Result appears", expectedContains: "12", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Why Test Your Code: A unit test checks one small piece of code, usually a single function, against known inputs and expected outputs.", approach: "Work in small steps for Why Test Your Code: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Why Test Your Code, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
