            exercise: { title: "Compute the sum for Writing a First Test", type: "build-function", instructions: "Write a C program that reads two integers from standard input and prints their sum. This practises Writing a First Test in Unit Testing in C: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a, b;\n    if (scanf(\"%d %d\", &a, &b) != 2) return 1;\n    /* TODO: print the result */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a, b;\n    if (scanf(\"%d %d\", &a, &b) != 2) return 1;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", a + b);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program reads two integers from standard input and prints their sum and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "17 5\n", expectedStdout: "22", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "20 10\n", expectedStdout: "30", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Writing a First Test: A first test calls the function with a known input, compares the result with the expected value, and prints a clear pass or fail message.", approach: "Work in small steps for Writing a First Test: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Writing a First Test, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
