            slug: "c-unit-testing-m1-l4",
            title: "Testing Edge Cases",
            concept: "Edge cases are the inputs at the boundaries of what a function accepts, such as zero, negative numbers, empty strings and the largest value. Bugs live at the boundaries far more often than in the middle of the range.",
            syntax: "/* test 0, 1, -1, and the maximum value */",
            exampleCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nstatic int fib(int n) {\n    if (n < 2) return n;\n    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(\"fib(8): %d\\n\", fib(8));\n    return 0;\n}\n",
            exampleLanguage: "c",
            mistake: "Testing only the happy path with a typical middle-of-the-range input.",
            bug: { prompt: "The code below opens a file but never closes it. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "FILE *f = fopen(\"data.txt\", \"r\");\nif (f == NULL) return 1;\nchar line[128];\nfgets(line, sizeof line, f);\nprintf(\"%s\", line);", fixedCode: "FILE *f = fopen(\"data.txt\", \"r\");\nif (f == NULL) return 1;\nchar line[128];\nfgets(line, sizeof line, f);\nprintf(\"%s\", line);\nfclose(f);", explanation: "Every successful fopen needs a matching fclose, otherwise the file descriptor and buffer leak." },
            exercise: { title: "Count the vowels for Testing Edge Cases", type: "write-code", instructions: "Write a C program that prints how many vowels a word contains. This practises Testing Edge Cases in Unit Testing in C: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *word = \"testing_edge\";\n    /* TODO: print the vowel count */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *word = \"testing_edge\";\n    int count = 0;\n    for (size_t i = 0; word[i] != '\\0'; i++) {\n        char c = word[i];\n        if (c=='a'||c=='e'||c=='i'||c=='o'||c=='u') count++;\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", count);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program prints how many vowels a word contains and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Output", expectedStdout: "4", hidden: false }, { name: "Result appears", expectedContains: "4", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Testing Edge Cases: Edge cases are the inputs at the boundaries of what a function accepts, such as zero, negative numbers, empty strings and the largest value.", approach: "Work in small steps for Testing Edge Cases: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Testing Edge Cases, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
