            bug: { prompt: "The code below copies a string into a fixed buffer without checking its length. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "char buf[16];\nstrcpy(buf, input);", fixedCode: "char buf[16];\nstrncpy(buf, input, sizeof(buf) - 1);\nbuf[sizeof(buf) - 1] = '\\0';", explanation: "strcpy copies without a length limit and can overflow buf; strncpy with an explicit bound plus a terminating NUL keeps the copy inside the buffer." },
            exercise: { title: "Find the maximum for Testing Functions that Return Values", type: "test-driven", instructions: "Write a C program that prints the largest value in a small fixed array. This practises Testing Functions that Return Values in Unit Testing in C: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    /* TODO: print the maximum value */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int values[] = {4, 9, 14, 19, 7};\n    int max = values[0];\n    for (int i = 1; i < 5; i++) if (values[i] > max) max = values[i];\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", max);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program prints the largest value in a small fixed array and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Output", expectedStdout: "19", hidden: false }, { name: "Result appears", expectedContains: "19", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Testing Functions that Return Values: Testing a function that returns a value means calling it with several inputs and comparing each result with the expected output.", approach: "Work in small steps for Testing Functions that Return Values: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Testing Functions that Return Values, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
