            exampleLanguage: "c",
            mistake: "Testing pointer code only for correct values and never for memory errors.",
            bug: { prompt: "The code below uses a variable before giving it a value. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "int total;\nfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) total += a[i];\nprintf(\"%d\\n\", total);", fixedCode: "int total = 0;\nfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) total += a[i];\nprintf(\"%d\\n\", total);", explanation: "An uninitialised local variable holds an indeterminate value, so total must be set to 0 before the loop accumulates into it." },
            exercise: { title: "Factorial for Testing Pointer Code", type: "write-code", instructions: "Write a C program that reads an integer n from standard input and prints n factorial. This practises Testing Pointer Code in Unit Testing in C: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    /* TODO: print the factorial */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    int r = 1;\n    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) r *= i;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", r);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program reads an integer n from standard input and prints n factorial and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "7\n", expectedStdout: "5040", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "9\n", expectedStdout: "362880", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Testing Pointer Code: Testing pointer code means checking that the function returns the right values and that it does not leak, double-free, or write out of bounds.", approach: "Work in small steps for Testing Pointer Code: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Testing Pointer Code, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
