            mistake: "Writing the code first and then writing a test that cannot fail.",
            bug: { prompt: "The code below prints a long value with the wrong format specifier. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "long count = 4000000000L;\nprintf(\"%d\\n\", count);", fixedCode: "long count = 4000000000L;\nprintf(\"%ld\\n\", count);", explanation: "The %d specifier expects an int, so a long must be printed with %ld or the value is misread." },
            exercise: { title: "Check your understanding of Test-Driven Development", type: "multiple-choice", instructions: "Which statement about Test-Driven Development is correct?", starterCode: "", solution: "Test-driven development means writing a failing test first, then writing the smallest code that makes it pass, and then refactoring.", solutionExplanation: "Test-driven development means writing a failing test first, then writing the smallest code that makes it pass, and then refactoring.", tests: [{ name: "Correct answer", expectedContains: "Test-driven development means writing a failing test first, then writing the smallest code that makes it pass, and then refactoring.", hidden: false }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Test-Driven Development: Test-driven development means writing a failing test first, then writing the smallest code that makes it pass, and then refactoring.", approach: "Work in small steps for Test-Driven Development: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Test-Driven Development, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." }, options: ["Test-Driven Development can only be used once in a program", "Test-Driven Development requires a separate paid licence to use", "Test-Driven Development is not available in C", "Test-driven development means writing a failing test first, then writing the smallest code that makes it pass, and then refactoring."], answerIndex: 3 },
