            exercise: { title: "Find the larger value for Declaring Function Pointers", type: "build-function", instructions: "Write a C program that reads two integers from standard input and prints the larger one. This practises Declaring Function Pointers in Function Pointers: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a, b;\n    if (scanf(\"%d %d\", &a, &b) != 2) return 1;\n    /* TODO: print the larger value */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a, b;\n    if (scanf(\"%d %d\", &a, &b) != 2) return 1;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", a > b ? a : b);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program reads two integers from standard input and prints the larger one and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "25 8\n", expectedStdout: "25", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "28 13\n", expectedStdout: "28", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Declaring Function Pointers: Declaring a function pointer means writing the return type, then (*name) in parentheses, then the parameter list, which is easy to get wrong without practice.", approach: "Work in small steps for Declaring Function Pointers: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Declaring Function Pointers, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
