            title: "Callbacks",
            concept: "A callback is a function passed to another function so it can be called when something happens, such as when an event arrives or a task completes. Callbacks let a library do its work while the application decides what happens at each step.",
            syntax: "on_event(callback); /* library calls callback later */",
            exampleCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nstatic int fib(int n) {\n    if (n < 2) return n;\n    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(\"fib(8): %d\\n\", fib(8));\n    return 0;\n}\n",
            exampleLanguage: "c",
            mistake: "Assuming a callback runs immediately instead of when the event happens.",
