            exercise: { title: "Compute the product for Callbacks", type: "write-code", instructions: "Write a C program that reads two integers from standard input and prints their product. This practises Callbacks in Function Pointers: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a, b;\n    if (scanf(\"%d %d\", &a, &b) != 2) return 1;\n    /* TODO: print the result */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a, b;\n    if (scanf(\"%d %d\", &a, &b) != 2) return 1;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", a * b);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program reads two integers from standard input and prints their product and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "11 10\n", expectedStdout: "110", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "14 15\n", expectedStdout: "210", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Callbacks: A callback is a function passed to another function so it can be called when something happens, such as when an event arrives or a task completes.", approach: "Work in small steps for Callbacks: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Callbacks, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
