            exercise: { title: "Fibonacci for Function Pointers vs Switch", type: "test-driven", instructions: "Write a C program that reads an integer n from standard input and prints the nth Fibonacci number (F(0)=0, F(1)=1). This practises Function Pointers vs Switch in Function Pointers: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    /* TODO: print the Fibonacci number */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    int a = 0, b = 1;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { int t = a + b; a = b; b = t; }\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", a);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program reads an integer n from standard input and prints the nth Fibonacci number (F(0)=0, F(1)=1) and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "10\n", expectedStdout: "55", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "12\n", expectedStdout: "144", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Function Pointers vs Switch: A dispatch table of function pointers is easier to extend than a switch, because adding an operation means adding an entry instead of editing a long chain of cases.", approach: "Work in small steps for Function Pointers vs Switch: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Function Pointers vs Switch, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
