            title: "Unions vs Structs",
            concept: "A struct holds all of its fields at once, while a union holds only one of its members at a time in shared memory. The size of a union is the size of its largest member, not the sum of all of them.",
            syntax: "/* struct: all fields; union: one member at a time */",
            exampleCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int total = 0;\n    for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) total += i;\n    printf(\"total: %d\\n\", total);\n    return 0;\n}\n",
            exampleLanguage: "c",
