            exercise: { title: "Length of a word for Unions vs Structs", type: "write-code", instructions: "Write a C program that prints the length of a word. This practises Unions vs Structs in Structs and Unions in Depth: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *word = \"unions_vs_st\";\n    /* TODO: print the length */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *word = \"unions_vs_st\";\n    printf(\"%zu\\n\", strlen(word));\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program prints the length of a word and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Output", expectedStdout: "12", hidden: false }, { name: "Result appears", expectedContains: "12", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Unions vs Structs: A struct holds all of its fields at once, while a union holds only one of its members at a time in shared memory.", approach: "Work in small steps for Unions vs Structs: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Unions vs Structs, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
