            exercise: { title: "Checksum of a word for Bit Fields in Structs", type: "test-driven", instructions: "Write a C program that prints the checksum (sum of the character codes) of a word. This practises Bit Fields in Structs in Structs and Unions in Depth: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *word = \"bit_fields_i\";\n    /* TODO: print the checksum */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *word = \"bit_fields_i\";\n    int total = 0;\n    for (size_t i = 0; word[i] != '\\0'; i++) total += (unsigned char)word[i];\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", total);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program prints the checksum (sum of the character codes) of a word and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Output", expectedStdout: "1245", hidden: false }, { name: "Result appears", expectedContains: "1245", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Bit Fields in Structs: A bit field is a struct member with a declared width in bits, which packs several small values into one word of memory.", approach: "Work in small steps for Bit Fields in Structs: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Bit Fields in Structs, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
