    capstoneExercise: { title: "Capstone coding task: Command-Line Arguments and Parsing", type: "build-program", instructions: "Write a complete C program applying what you learned in Command-Line Arguments and Parsing. It should reads an integer n from standard input and prints 2 to the power n. Print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    /* TODO: print the power of two */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    int r = 1;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) r *= 2;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", r);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program reads an integer n from standard input and prints 2 to the power n and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "5\n", expectedStdout: "32", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "7\n", expectedStdout: "128", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "The capstone for Command-Line Arguments and Parsing combines the track's ideas into one program: read the input, compute with a loop, and print only the result.", approach: "Work in three steps: read the input, compute the value, then print the result.", structure: "Keep the program in one main function with clearly initialised variables, and test it with the example input first." } },
