            title: "Conditional Compilation",
            concept: "Conditional compilation with #if, #ifdef and #ifndef includes or excludes parts of the source depending on which macros are defined. It is how one codebase supports several platforms or optional features.",
            syntax: "#ifdef DEBUG\nprintf(\"debug: %d\\n\", x);\n#endif",
            exampleCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int values[] = {4, 9, 14, 19, 7};\n    int max = values[0];\n    for (int i = 1; i < 5; i++) if (values[i] > max) max = values[i];\n    printf(\"max: %d\\n\", max);\n    return 0;\n}\n",
            exampleLanguage: "c",
            mistake: "Leaving debug code in the program because the #ifdef was never closed.",
            bug: { prompt: "The code below should compute the average of two integers, but it prints 0 or a truncated value. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "int avg = (a + b) / 2;\nprintf(\"%d\\n\", avg);", fixedCode: "double avg = (a + b) / 2.0;\nprintf(\"%f\\n\", avg);", explanation: "Integer division truncates before the result is stored, so at least one operand must be a double to keep the fractional part." },
            exercise: { title: "Check your understanding of Conditional Compilation", type: "multiple-choice", instructions: "Which statement about Conditional Compilation is correct?", starterCode: "", solution: "Conditional compilation with #if, #ifdef and #ifndef includes or excludes parts of the source depending on which macros are defined.", solutionExplanation: "Conditional compilation with #if, #ifdef and #ifndef includes or excludes parts of the source depending on which macros are defined.", tests: [{ name: "Correct answer", expectedContains: "Conditional compilation with #if, #ifdef and #ifndef includes or excludes parts of the source depending on which macros are defined.", hidden: false }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Conditional Compilation: Conditional compilation with #if, #ifdef and #ifndef includes or excludes parts of the source depending on which macros are defined.", approach: "Work in small steps for Conditional Compilation: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Conditional Compilation, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." }, options: ["Conditional compilation with #if, #ifdef and #ifndef includes or excludes parts of the source depending on which macros are defined.", "Conditional Compilation can only be used once in a program", "Conditional Compilation requires a separate paid licence to use", "Conditional Compilation is not available in C"], answerIndex: 0 },
