            exercise: { title: "Power of two for Include Guards Revisited", type: "test-driven", instructions: "Write a C program that reads an integer n from standard input and prints 2 to the power n. This practises Include Guards Revisited in The C Preprocessor: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    /* TODO: print the power of two */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    int r = 1;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) r *= 2;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", r);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program reads an integer n from standard input and prints 2 to the power n and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "5\n", expectedStdout: "32", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "7\n", expectedStdout: "128", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Include Guards Revisited: An include guard stops a header from being included twice in one translation unit, which would otherwise cause duplicate definitions.", approach: "Work in small steps for Include Guards Revisited: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Include Guards Revisited, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
