    capstoneExercise: { title: "Capstone coding task: The C Preprocessor", type: "build-program", instructions: "Write a complete C program applying what you learned in The C Preprocessor. It should reads a positive integer from standard input and prints the sum of its digits. Print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    /* TODO: print the digit sum */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    int s = 0;\n    for (int x = n; x > 0; x /= 10) s += x % 10;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", s);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program reads a positive integer from standard input and prints the sum of its digits and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "322\n", expectedStdout: "7", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "324\n", expectedStdout: "9", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "The capstone for The C Preprocessor combines the track's ideas into one program: read the input, compute with a loop, and print only the result.", approach: "Work in three steps: read the input, compute the value, then print the result.", structure: "Keep the program in one main function with clearly initialised variables, and test it with the example input first." } },
