            title: "String Ownership",
            concept: "String ownership means knowing which part of the program is responsible for freeing a string, and the rule must be written down for every function that returns one. Unclear ownership is the most common cause of string leaks and double frees.",
            syntax: "/* the caller owns the returned string and must free it */",
            exampleCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *word = \"example\";\n    printf(\"length of %s: %zu\\n\", word, strlen(word));\n    return 0;\n}\n",
            exampleLanguage: "c",
            mistake: "Returning a string without saying who is responsible for freeing it.",
            bug: { prompt: "The code below should process every element of an array of size n, but it reads past the end. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {\n    total += buf[i];\n}", fixedCode: "for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n    total += buf[i];\n}", explanation: "The loop condition must be i < n, because valid indices are 0 to n - 1; i <= n reads buf[n], one past the end." },
            exercise: { title: "Reverse a word for String Ownership", type: "test-driven", instructions: "Write a C program that prints a word reversed. This practises String Ownership in Dynamic Strings: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *word = \"string_owner\";\n    /* TODO: print the word reversed */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *word = \"string_owner\";\n    size_t n = strlen(word);\n    for (size_t i = n; i > 0; i--) putchar(word[i - 1]);\n    putchar('\\n');\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program prints a word reversed and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Output", expectedStdout: "renwo_gnirts", hidden: false }, { name: "Result appears", expectedContains: "renwo_gnirts", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of String Ownership: String ownership means knowing which part of the program is responsible for freeing a string, and the rule must be written down for every function that returns one.", approach: "Work in small steps for String Ownership: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for String Ownership, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
