            exercise: { title: "Triangular numbers for Dynamic Strings in Practice", type: "write-code", instructions: "Write a C program that reads an integer n from standard input and prints 1 + 2 + ... + n. This practises Dynamic Strings in Practice in Dynamic Strings: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    /* TODO: print the triangular number */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    int total = 0;\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) total += i;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", total);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program reads an integer n from standard input and prints 1 + 2 + ... + n and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "11\n", expectedStdout: "66", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "13\n", expectedStdout: "91", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Dynamic Strings in Practice: In practice, dynamic strings mean allocating with room for the terminator, growing by doubling, copying safely, and documenting ownership.", approach: "Work in small steps for Dynamic Strings in Practice: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Dynamic Strings in Practice, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
