            bug: { prompt: "The code below opens a file but never closes it. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "FILE *f = fopen(\"data.txt\", \"r\");\nif (f == NULL) return 1;\nchar line[128];\nfgets(line, sizeof line, f);\nprintf(\"%s\", line);", fixedCode: "FILE *f = fopen(\"data.txt\", \"r\");\nif (f == NULL) return 1;\nchar line[128];\nfgets(line, sizeof line, f);\nprintf(\"%s\", line);\nfclose(f);", explanation: "Every successful fopen needs a matching fclose, otherwise the file descriptor and buffer leak." },
            exercise: { title: "Checksum of a word for Deleting from a Circular List", type: "write-code", instructions: "Write a C program that prints the checksum (sum of the character codes) of a word. This practises Deleting from a Circular List in Circular Lists and Deques: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *word = \"deleting_fro\";\n    /* TODO: print the checksum */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *word = \"deleting_fro\";\n    int total = 0;\n    for (size_t i = 0; word[i] != '\\0'; i++) total += (unsigned char)word[i];\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", total);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program prints the checksum (sum of the character codes) of a word and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Output", expectedStdout: "1266", hidden: false }, { name: "Result appears", expectedContains: "1266", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Deleting from a Circular List: Deleting from a circular list means linking the predecessor around the removed node and, if the removed node was the starting node, choosing a new start.", approach: "Work in small steps for Deleting from a Circular List: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Deleting from a Circular List, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
