            bug: { prompt: "The code below copies a string into a fixed buffer without checking its length. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "char buf[16];\nstrcpy(buf, input);", fixedCode: "char buf[16];\nstrncpy(buf, input, sizeof(buf) - 1);\nbuf[sizeof(buf) - 1] = '\\0';", explanation: "strcpy copies without a length limit and can overflow buf; strncpy with an explicit bound plus a terminating NUL keeps the copy inside the buffer." },
            exercise: { title: "Fibonacci for Array-Based Deques", type: "test-driven", instructions: "Write a C program that reads an integer n from standard input and prints the nth Fibonacci number (F(0)=0, F(1)=1). This practises Array-Based Deques in Circular Lists and Deques: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    /* TODO: print the Fibonacci number */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    int a = 0, b = 1;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { int t = a + b; a = b; b = t; }\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", a);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program reads an integer n from standard input and prints the nth Fibonacci number (F(0)=0, F(1)=1) and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "11\n", expectedStdout: "89", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "13\n", expectedStdout: "233", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Array-Based Deques: An array-based deque is a circular buffer with front and rear indices that can move in either direction, wrapping around with modulo arithmetic.", approach: "Work in small steps for Array-Based Deques: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Array-Based Deques, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
