            exercise: { title: "Sum of digits for Deques vs Queues", type: "test-driven", instructions: "Write a C program that reads a positive integer from standard input and prints the sum of its digits. This practises Deques vs Queues in Circular Lists and Deques: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    /* TODO: print the digit sum */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    int s = 0;\n    for (int x = n; x > 0; x /= 10) s += x % 10;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", s);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program reads a positive integer from standard input and prints the sum of its digits and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "211\n", expectedStdout: "4", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "213\n", expectedStdout: "6", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Deques vs Queues: A deque can do everything a queue can do, plus operations at the other end, but it costs extra code and extra care.", approach: "Work in small steps for Deques vs Queues: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Deques vs Queues, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
