            exercise: { title: "Square a number for Why Complexity Matters", type: "write-code", instructions: "Write a C program that reads an integer from standard input and prints its square. This practises Why Complexity Matters in Algorithm Complexity and Big-O: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    /* TODO: print n squared */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) != 1) return 1;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", n * n);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program reads an integer from standard input and prints its square and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "10\n", expectedStdout: "100", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "12\n", expectedStdout: "144", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Why Complexity Matters: Complexity analysis predicts how an algorithm behaves as its input grows, which matters because a program that is fast on ten items can be unusable on a million.", approach: "Work in small steps for Why Complexity Matters: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Why Complexity Matters, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
