            mistake: "Reading O(n) as a promise that the program runs in exactly n steps.",
            bug: { prompt: "The code below allocates memory but never checks whether the allocation failed. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "int *p = malloc(n * sizeof(int));\np[0] = 42;", fixedCode: "int *p = malloc(n * sizeof(int));\nif (p == NULL) {\n    return 1;\n}\np[0] = 42;", explanation: "malloc can return NULL when allocation fails, so the pointer must be checked before it is dereferenced." },
            exercise: { title: "Check your understanding of Big-O Notation", type: "multiple-choice", instructions: "Which statement about Big-O Notation is correct?", starterCode: "", solution: "Big-O notation describes how an algorithm's cost grows, ignoring constant factors and lower-order terms, so O(n log n) means the cost grows almost linearly.", solutionExplanation: "Big-O notation describes how an algorithm's cost grows, ignoring constant factors and lower-order terms, so O(n log n) means the cost grows almost linearly.", tests: [{ name: "Correct answer", expectedContains: "Big-O notation describes how an algorithm's cost grows, ignoring constant factors and lower-order terms, so O(n log n) means the cost grows almost linearly.", hidden: false }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Big-O Notation: Big-O notation describes how an algorithm's cost grows, ignoring constant factors and lower-order terms, so O(n log n) means the cost grows almost linearly.", approach: "Work in small steps for Big-O Notation: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Big-O Notation, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." }, options: ["Big-O Notation can only be used once in a program", "Big-O Notation requires a separate paid licence to use", "Big-O notation describes how an algorithm's cost grows, ignoring constant factors and lower-order terms, so O(n log n) means the cost grows almost linearly.", "Big-O Notation is not available in C"], answerIndex: 2 },
