            title: "Space Complexity",
            concept: "Space complexity measures how much extra memory an algorithm needs as its input grows, in the same Big-O terms as time. Merge sort needs linear extra space, while quick sort needs only logarithmic stack space.",
            syntax: "/* merge sort: O(n) extra space; quick sort: O(log n) */",
            exampleCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int total = 0;\n    for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) total += i;\n    printf(\"total: %d\\n\", total);\n    return 0;\n}\n",
            exampleLanguage: "c",
            mistake: "Ignoring memory use and running out of RAM on large inputs.",
            bug: { prompt: "The code below uses a variable before giving it a value. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "int total;\nfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) total += a[i];\nprintf(\"%d\\n\", total);", fixedCode: "int total = 0;\nfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) total += a[i];\nprintf(\"%d\\n\", total);", explanation: "An uninitialised local variable holds an indeterminate value, so total must be set to 0 before the loop accumulates into it." },
            exercise: { title: "Compute the product for Space Complexity", type: "write-code", instructions: "Write a C program that reads two integers from standard input and prints their product. This practises Space Complexity in Algorithm Complexity and Big-O: follow the steps in the starter code and print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a, b;\n    if (scanf(\"%d %d\", &a, &b) != 2) return 1;\n    /* TODO: print the result */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a, b;\n    if (scanf(\"%d %d\", &a, &b) != 2) return 1;\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", a * b);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program reads two integers from standard input and prints their product and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Example input", stdin: "14 9\n", expectedStdout: "126", hidden: false }, { name: "Second input", stdin: "17 14\n", expectedStdout: "238", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "Remember the key idea of Space Complexity: Space complexity measures how much extra memory an algorithm needs as its input grows, in the same Big-O terms as time.", approach: "Work in small steps for Space Complexity: break the task into reading the input, computing the value, and printing only the result in the exact format shown.", structure: "Start from the starter code for Space Complexity, fill in the missing computation, then run it and compare the output with the expected value." } },
