    capstoneExercise: { title: "Capstone coding task: Algorithm Complexity and Big-O", type: "build-program", instructions: "Write a complete C program applying what you learned in Algorithm Complexity and Big-O. It should prints the checksum (sum of the character codes) of a word. Print only the result.", starterCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *word = \"algorithm_co\";\n    /* TODO: print the checksum */\n    return 0;\n}\n", solution: "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *word = \"algorithm_co\";\n    int total = 0;\n    for (size_t i = 0; word[i] != '\\0'; i++) total += (unsigned char)word[i];\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", total);\n    return 0;\n}\n", solutionExplanation: "The program prints the checksum (sum of the character codes) of a word and prints only the result.", tests: [{ name: "Output", expectedStdout: "1272", hidden: false }, { name: "Result appears", expectedContains: "1272", hidden: true }], hintNotes: { concept: "The capstone for Algorithm Complexity and Big-O combines the track's ideas into one program: read the input, compute with a loop, and print only the result.", approach: "Work in three steps: read the input, compute the value, then print the result.", structure: "Keep the program in one main function with clearly initialised variables, and test it with the example input first." } },
